    "q-100624": {
      "dom_id": "step-q100624",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14153326/assets/38b8cedf7155418e898f4bbd1c5aefd4c9be7bc8fa93efbd6b15042bc2177cb3.png)\n\nUse the model above to determine the missing number in the equation below.\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{5}÷\\frac{9}{10}=\\frac{\\boxed{\\,\\,}}{9}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "The denominators are $5$ and $10.$ We can make a common denominator of $10$ by splitting the shape on the left into $2$ parts vertically: \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14153326/assets/9792dd036c4de182d4cd491830bbca85d083b7b19e80c356e67615d8f00db230.png)\n\n \n\nSo, our division problem now is\n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{10}÷\\frac{9}{10}.\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are equal, this is equivalent to\n\n$$\n8÷9=\\frac{8}{9}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{5}÷\\frac{9}{10}=\\frac{\\boxed{\\,8\\,}}{9}\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $8.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
