    "q-100723": {
      "dom_id": "step-q100723",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14153326/assets/de7182658c41542b8e6ef212aa1896204c4c4fe07a79c8ef93f85012188684d6.png)\n\nFind the value of $\\frac{3}{8}÷\\frac{2}{4}$ given the model above.",
      "worked_solution": "The denominators are $8$ and $4.$ We can make a common denominator of $8.$\n\nWe split the shape on the right of the division sign into $2$ parts horizontally:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14153326/assets/f9bb3e4777085c1e02c4965c41972f5e95261b1228f84b567d049bee8bbe139e.png)\n\nSo, our division problem now is\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{8}÷\\frac{4}{8}.\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are equal, this is equivalent to\n\n$$\n3÷4=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{8}÷\\frac{2}{4}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
