    "q-92839": {
      "dom_id": "step-q92839",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14153326/assets/2ef3a4174cf69597dcbc0e7d403c8fc8c52c8db12a4d2cf3e49c68571888a1d9.png)\n\nFind the value of $\\frac{1}{3}÷\\frac{3}{4}$ given the model above.",
      "worked_solution": "The denominators are $3$ and $4.$ We can make a common denominator of $3\\times 4=12.$ \n\nWe split the shape on the left of the division sign into $4$ parts vertically, and we split the shape on the right into $3$ parts horizontally:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14153326/assets/07693bef459c0417056470494462c411f368f88900d117f48ee155e2c6bba52b.png)\n\nSo, our division problem now is\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{12}÷\\frac{9}{12}.\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are equal, this is equivalent to\n\n$$\n4÷9=\\frac{4}{9}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}÷\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{4}{9}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
